00:04:42.000 --> 00:04:52.000
Uh, when we build… when we build digital identity systems, we assume one entity controls one identity through a valid chain of cryptographic keys.

00:04:52.000 --> 00:04:58.000
Keys may rotate over time, but the history of control should remain consistent.

00:04:58.000 --> 00:05:02.000
Two major risks threaten that assumption. Impersonation and duplicity.

00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:05.000
Let's see…

00:05:05.000 --> 00:05:16.000
Impersonation happens when someone steals your keys. They can act as you and continue updating your identity, even though you've lost control.

00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:22.000
Duplicity is different. It happens when the identity owner creates two conflicting versions of their own history.

00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:25.000
Showing one version to one party, and another version elsewhere.

00:05:25.000 --> 00:05:30.000
Both appear valid independently, but they cannot both be true.

00:05:30.000 --> 00:05:34.000
Traditionally, we solve these problems…

00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:42.000
Traditionally, we solve these problems using trusted third parties. A wedding certificate, for example, includes witnesses

00:05:42.000 --> 00:05:44.000
who independently confirm what happened.

00:05:44.000 --> 00:05:55.000
Later, blockchains extended this idea by acting as global… as a global witness. Everyone shares the same history.

00:05:55.000 --> 00:06:04.000
new decentralized identity systems, like did WebVH and did WebS use a different model based on watchers and witnesses?

00:06:04.000 --> 00:06:11.000
Witnesses are selected by the identity owner. Whenever the identity changes, witnesses record the event and issue

00:06:11.000 --> 00:06:21.000
cryptographic receipts proving they observed it. They don't control the identity, they simply provide an independent record of history.

00:06:21.000 --> 00:06:31.000
This helps both impersonation and duplicity. Conflicting histories or suspicious changes become visible because witnesses have recorded what they actually saw.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:38.000
Watchers add another layer. They are chosen by the verifier, not the identity owner.

00:06:38.000 --> 00:06:44.000
Watchers compare an identity's history against witness records and look for inconsistencies.

00:06:44.000 --> 00:06:55.000
Together, witnesses and watchers create decentralized accountability. Instead of relying on a central authority, trust comes from transparent records and independent verification.

00:06:55.000 --> 00:07:01.000
The goal is not to prevent every attack in advance, but to ensure that impersonation or duplicity

00:07:01.000 --> 00:07:06.000
can be detected and proven when it occurs.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:09.000
So, that's the…

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:15.000
the bite-sized trust for duplicity and impersonation.

